  "query_preview": "..\n\nA compact mid level low will track from southern Saskatchewan to \nnorthern Ontario today while the mid level ridge axis over Lower Mi \nexits east of the region. East-southeasterly low level wind fields \nacross Se Mi early this morning will veer toward the south as a \nbroad low to mid level anticyclone departs east. This will \ncorrespond to an influx of warmer air into the forecast area today. \nIncreased cloud cover through the day associated with moisture \nadvection around 850mb and increasing mid/high clouds from the west \nwill result in overall increasing cloud trends, likely capping \nafternoon highs to the lower 80s. \n\nLatest water vapor imagery across North America this morning shows a \nplume of moisture off the Pacific advecting into the Upper Midwest \nwhile Gulf moisture in the lower levels feeds into this same region. \nThis is leading to a pool of very deep moisture; latest SPC meso \nanalysis indicates precipitable water values of 1.7 to 2.1 inches \nextending across the central plains to upper Midwest. This deep layer\nmoist axis will advance across Lower Mi tonight as the ridge axis \nflattens and the mean flow upstream turns more westerly. The \ncorresponding increase in mid level moisture will support increasing \nshower chances during the night, mainly across the Saginaw Valley and\nthumb. \n\nForecast strengthening of an upper jet streak across the northern \nlakes Wednesday will support an expanding region of upper divergence \nand weak mid level frontal forcing across northern and central Lower\nMi. The forcing within the deep moist axis will sustain rain chances\nthrough Wednesday as the mid level frontal boundary sinks southward \ninto srn Lower Mi. One point of uncertainty to the Wednesday forecast\ninvolves the remnants of a potential convective complex expected to \ndevelop over ern Iowa/nrn Illinois tonight. An MCV from this feature \nwould certainly enhance precip chances across srn Mi if it were to \nmaterialize. A deeper/slower mid level wave may also support a better\ninflux of instability into the far southern portions of the forecast\narea Wed afternoon, supported by a few high res solutions. This in \nturn has allowed Wed CAPE probabilities to trend upward south of the\nI-94 corridor. The SPC Day2 convective outlook has thus nudged the \narea of Marginal Risk into Monroe County. Given the degree of deep \nlayer moisture overhead Wednesday, if deep convection does \nmaterialize, intense rainfall rates will be of concern. \n\nThere is strong agreement that the deep layer moist axis and mid \nlevel frontal boundary will remain progressive as mid level west-\nnorthwest flow expands across the northern Great Lakes by Thursday, \nadvecting a much drier airmass across Se Mi. Model soundings \nindicate subtle cold air advection will be offset by steep low level \nlapse rates to boost Thurs highs well into the 70s. Mid level \nridging and associated surface high pressure will then build across \nthe region, supporting dry conditions to end the work week.",
